  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1223 AM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nToday: Rain Shower Potential\n\nAs an upper level cutoff low churning over the High Plains slowly \nshifts eastward through the early morning hours, scattered showers \nare expected to develop across the Upper Mississippi River Valley. \nForcing for these showers is weak at best, primarily driven by an \narea of 1000-500hPa Q-Vector convergence. However, some convective \nenhancement is possible this afternoon given 25-50 J/kg of 3CAPE \nassociated with slightly steepened 900-800hPa lapse rates seen in \nRAP/RRFS soundings. Given the weak forcing over much of the area and \nthe track of the upper low, thinking the greatest potential for \nscattered showers exists along and south of I-90 today (40-70%). \n21.00z CAM solutions are beginning to suggest that those north of I-\n90 could see some scattered shower development this afternoon as \nwell (10-30%) given the steeper low level lapse rates, though \nshallower moisture with northward extent would likely keep any \ndevelopment fairly isolated. \n\nAs far as amounts are concerned, expectations remain on the lower \nend around 1/4 inch at most, although there is a low signal for \nlocalized amounts near 1/2 inch across northeast Iowa and southwest \nWisconsin in the 21.00z HREF (10-20%) if any convective enhancement \noccurs. \n\nTuesday - Friday: Staying Cool and Drier\n\nBeyond today, northeasterly surface flow and high pressure \nsouth of the Hudson Bay will allow for cooler and drier \nconditions to advect into our region. As a result, median high \ntemperatures in the NBM throughout the week generally remain in \nthe 60s for most of the local area. As we head into Wednesday \nmorning, upper-level ridging will push into the local area which\nin turn should both allow for a gradual warming trend and \nclearing skies during this period. Consequently, not seeing much\nfor precipitation chances till later into the week when maybe \nsome shortwave perturbations try to sneak back into the region \nduring the Thursday and Friday timeframe. However, \npredictability with these waves remains very low at this time. \nIn any case, will have to watch Wednesday morning for some frost\npotential across north-central WI with median low temperatures \nin the middle to upper 30s.",
